Yes. This gets interesting fast.

I did it as a paper simulation of daemon play using the daemon roles I can reconstruct confidently—especially Spider = graph-weaver, Hound = anomaly-hunter, Mirror = verification/invariant guard—plus Pantheon’s existing Goal-Seek / reconstruction mechanics. I’m treating the less-explicit daemon behavior as provisional rather than pretending we have a complete executable spec.

Experiment A — Free Play

Rule:

Pick one Narrative Gym kata + one Deal Gym kata.
No objective. Follow whatever structural resemblance appears interesting.
Preserve useful mutations.

Draw 1

Hero’s Journey × Loan

Starting forms:

Hero:
Ordinary\ World\rightarrow Departure\rightarrow Trials\rightarrow Transformation\rightarrow Return

Loan:
Value_{now}\rightarrow Obligation\rightarrow Repayment_{future}

Spider immediately sees:

Gift_{initial}\rightarrow Debt\rightarrow Trials\rightarrow Repayment/Return

And suddenly a huge number of stories become credit structures.

The hero receives something before earning it:

* sword
* knowledge
* inheritance
* power
* mercy
* second chance
* trust

Then the narrative asks whether the hero can become the person capable of repaying the advance.

So:

Heroic\ Arc
\approx
Credit\ extended\ against\ future\ character

That gives us a new operator:

def narrative_credit(character, gift):
    grant(gift, before=worthiness_proven)
    while not mature(character):
        impose_trials(character)
    return repay(
        gift,
        medium="service / sacrifice / return / transmission"
    )

Kata: Find what the protagonist receives before they deserve it. What constitutes repayment?

That’s actually quite good.

