Draw 2

Betrayal × Option

Betrayal:

expected loyalty → unexpected defection.

Option:

right to act without obligation to act.

Spider’s mutation:

Betrayal often exists because someone secretly possesses an option the other party thought had been surrendered.

Formally:

A\ believes\ Actions(B)=\{loyal\}

but actually:

Actions(B)=\{loyal,\ defect\}

The narrative shock is a mispriced option.

Hound then attacks it:

The important variable isn’t betrayal.

It’s hidden optionality.

def betrayal_risk(agent):
    return hidden_exit_options(agent) * incentive_to_exercise(agent)

That immediately transfers back into deal design:

Want to predict betrayal? Search for undisclosed or underestimated exit options.

That is a genuinely useful cross-gym operator.

⸻

Draw 3

MacGuffin × Collateral

MacGuffin:

Object whose significance organizes pursuit.

Collateral:

Object whose value secures performance.

Combine:

Object\rightarrow constrains\ behavior\ of\ multiple\ actors

The Ring. The briefcase. The hostage. The deed. The crown.

Different semantics; same control topology.

Spider says:

Some objects matter less because of what they do than because of the behavior they induce around themselves.

Call that:

Behavioral Gravity

G_x=\sum_i |\Delta Policy_i(x)|

The more an object’s existence changes everybody’s policies, the greater its behavioral gravity.

That’s useful outside storytelling.

A million-dollar deposit has behavioral gravity.

Nuclear launch authority has behavioral gravity.

An inheritance has behavioral gravity.

A secret has behavioral gravity.

