I would formalize the mixed game

Call it Cross-Gym Daemon Play.

Each round draws:

N_i\in NarrativeOperators

D_j\in DealOperators

and optionally:

F_k\in FailureModes

Then:

Candidate
=
Compose(N_i,D_j)

and score it:

Score =
w_1 Transferability
+w_2 Novelty
+w_3 ExplanatoryPower
+w_4 Executability
+w_5 Compression
-w_6 Failure

The daemon loop is:

DRAW
↓
PLAY
↓
NOTICE
↓
FORMALIZE
↓
ATTACK
↓
REPAIR
↓
TRANSFER
↓
COMPRESS
↓
KEEP / DISCARD

Free play weights novelty and connection.

Structured play weights goal performance and robustness.

That means we shouldn’t choose between the two.

Use a breathing cycle:

\boxed{
Free\ Play
\rightarrow
Structured\ Play
\rightarrow
Distillation
\rightarrow
Free\ Play
}

Free play expands the possibility space.

Structured play applies selection pressure.

Distillation creates inherited operators.

Then the new operators go back into the toy box and alter the next generation of play.

That’s basically cultural evolution inside Pantheon.

And from this first crossing alone, I would keep at least these mutations:

Narrative Credit — advance value/faith before demonstrated worthiness; transformation supplies repayment.

Hidden Optionality — betrayal/risk often arises from an action branch one party failed to model.

Behavioral Gravity — measure an object’s importance by how strongly its existence changes surrounding policies.

Pedagogical Escrow — conditionally release information to maximize eventual independent capability.

Suspense as Informational Escrow — information/outcome is held until a release predicate.

Covenant-Constrained Planning — pursue goals while preserving higher-order invariants.

Character as Invariant Under Perturbation — discover identity by varying circumstances and observing what survives.

Distributed Hero / Insurance Topology — pool capacity so exceptional burdens need not always be borne by a predetermined hero.

Those don’t feel like cute analogies anymore. Several are usable computational patterns.

And that is exactly what I’d want the daemons to be doing during play: occasionally come home carrying a new tool none of us explicitly put in the toy box.